WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
It's a new day on Tesla Short's time, episode 553.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.960
It's July 26th, 2026, and I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.820
Here's what you need to know about Tesla today.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.180
Tesla's wafer scale AI design can now isolate an overheating

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.020
dye and keep the rest of the training fabric running

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.740
without engineer intervention.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.780
Each dye carries embedded sensors that report temperature,

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.080
voltage, current, power, workload, bandwidth, and latency,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.760
along with its wafer address.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.180
Microcontrollers at the edge filter the stream

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.580
and escalate only relevant readings to a central controller.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.560
When thresholds are crossed, the controller can throttle power,

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.260
deactivate the dye, or repartition compute fabric,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.500
so healthier sections absorb the load.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.600
Stored telemetry creates a live health map

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.600
and digital flight recorder for the entire data center.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.940
Signals from power and cooling infrastructure feed the same loop,

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.440
widening oversight from single dyes to full racks.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.640
The approach turns a local silicon fault

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.000
into a contained event rather than a system-wide throttle

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.680
or shutdown.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.620
A wafer scale processor packs thousands of dyes

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.080
so tightly that one local fault can throttle

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.660
or halt the entire array.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.700
Tesla's approach embeds sensors and a control fabric

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.860
that lets the system detect heat or voltage problems,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.280
isolate the affected dye and reallocate work

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.580
to the remaining silicon without waiting for human intervention.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.280
The result is a compute cluster that behaves more

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.960
like a resilient organism than a brittle machine

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.820
whose weakest square dictates overall performance.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.340
Every dye reports its own vital signs

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.740
through dedicated telemetry paths and microcontrollers

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.680
at the edge decide what data deserves escalation.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.100
When a threshold is crossed,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.600
the central controller can choke power, quarantine the dye,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.000
or hand its workload to neighboring healthy sections.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.120
That resilience matters because training runs

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.900
for FSD and Optimus stretch across weeks or months.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.580
By handling faults at the dye level,

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.080
the design reduces the frequency of full system restarts

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.140
and keeps utilization higher across the cortex

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.460
and dojo clusters.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.220
Higher sustained utilization means fewer racks

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:23.820
are needed to deliver the same training throughput,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.300
which directly lowers the cost per inference hour

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.140
and per robot training cycle.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.400
From keeping training clusters running reliably,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.200
we turned to how that training is already showing up

